A||||19||stsmithgt||double stick tape mess.  Suggestions?||||5||
Z||000000||stsmithgt||10-23-2000||06:56 PM||||Well I took my 86GT sail panels off to find a mess from the double stick tape.  Does anybody have a suggestion on what to use to get that old tape off without risk of ruining my paint?  I actually got my right panel off in one piece. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||PAUL88GT||10-23-2000||07:17 PM||||I used isopropyl alcohol and rubbed like hell for over an hour.  It is very hard to get off, but I was afraid to use stronger solvents to prevent harming the finish.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||10-23-2000||08:51 PM||||Maybe try a product called GOO-GONE or go to your local autopaint store and buy adhesive remover.<P>Good Luck!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||10-23-2000||10:37 PM||||A heat gun will soften the adhesive enough to roll most of the double stick off fairly painlessly, then much less elbow grease is required with the solvents.<BR>It doesn't have to be cooked, just warmed to where its pliable.||||reg||12
Z||000004||webbee||10-23-2000||10:54 PM||||I use lighter fluid. It is actually naptha. Safe for most surfaces. It will soffen/mar paint if you leave it on too long. A quick blast on a paper towel then on the glue will usually do the trick. Cheaper than GooGone or adhesive remover.<P>[This message has been edited by webbee (edited 10-23-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by webbee (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-23-2000||11:19 PM||||I use this stuff called "Oops". It works really good. I think you can get it at Wal-mart, and I know you can get it at Advanced Auto stores. Heat is great too.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||William||10-23-2000||11:37 PM||||Heres what I did, I took a single edge razor blade, held it at a slight angle and slowly glided it all the way around the panel then I cleaned up the remaining residue with lighter fluid. Before I used the blade, it took 30 minutes to do a 4 inch section. After switching to the razor blade the whole job on both side took about 10 minutes total. BIG difference! Be careful.........||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrPBody||10-24-2000||08:22 AM||||Ditto to the razor blade idea.  I used a sticker scraper, which holds a single-edge razor blade.  To remove uscrapable residue, try brake parts cleaner (CRC Brakleen).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||10-24-2000||09:30 AM||||Thanks for all the input guys.  I forgot about using heat.  I think I'll go that route.  I already have Goo Gone I could use for the final clean up.  Brake Cleaner? Sounds harsh, but maybe there might be a couple of spots too tough for Goo Gone to deal with.  I like the lighter fluid idea.<P> FIRE is cool especially when it goes BOOM!  <P>I didn't want to find myself with a rag of laquer thinner trying to get this stuff off.  Thanks again for your ideas.<BR>Todd!¡ <P>(freakin' server keeps dumping our LAN every five minutes.  Makes it tough to get out a message. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> )||||reg||3
Z||000009||Tigger||10-24-2000||09:33 AM||||Try 3M Trim Adhesive remover, comes in a spray can.  You should be able to find it at a local auto paint store.  It's made to release the 2 way tape on body trim and I believe it's safe and won't harm the paint.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||10-24-2000||10:22 AM||||I guess I am just lazy about it, but I just wound up taking my car to the coin op carwash and just pressure-blasted the crap right off. I was left with some residue, but that just gives the new tape something to stick to. The main thing is I had a clean surface to attach to.  Remember, water is a solvent... ||||reg||1
Z||000011||stsmithgt||10-24-2000||10:36 AM||||Trim adhiesive remover.  Well that makes sense.  That probably works better than Goo Gone. Thanks T||||reg||1
Z||000012||cowans||10-24-2000||01:55 PM||||Here's the best one yet!!! Try some WD 40! Let it sit 5 min...removes all sorts of sticky gum. Sandy||||reg||10
Z||000013||rogergarrison||10-25-2000||08:43 PM||||Heres what I do in my body shop... use razor blade to get off the bulk of tape. Go to body supply/paint store. 3M makes this little wheel made up from a bunch of rubber like disks. Just put in a drill or dremel tool and zip it off, no goo, paint scrapes, or risk of thinners or alcohol getting to your paint. all you have to clean up is rubberlike sanding dust. Works great to take off stripes and decals too.||||reg||1
Z||000014||2xsess||10-25-2000||09:22 PM||||Dang, I had forgeten about those 3m wheels, definitly the easiest and fastest way.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000015||stsmithgt||10-26-2000||10:18 AM||||Cool, like a rotory eraser.  Sounds like a gizmo to throw into my dremel set. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> Nothing like finding some new (or old) gizmo to collect.<p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 10-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Patrick's Dad||10-26-2000||10:47 AM||||The post that said lighter fluid got it right. It removes the adhesive without residue, and it just burns off (not with fire, in the air) without much fuss. Too much can burn the plastic, so use it sparingly.<P>We who recondition old cameras love our Ronsinol....<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fret Naught||10-26-2000||12:31 PM||||Lighter Fluid is the way to go, I use it all the time on guitars and other finely finished instruments. not to much sparingly on a rag works great last long time ||||reg||14
Z||000018||sombre||10-26-2000||11:55 PM||||<BR>    I own a vinyl sign business and have used 3 solvents to remove this type of tape depending on the surface it's on.<P>   first off heat and a "plastic" scaper will remove most of the heavy stuff with little effort.<P>   Then depending on the surface use<BR>   <BR>     PPG DX-440 wax and grease remover (avalable at any ppg autobody supply store) This is the weakest stuff made for prepping old paint for new or removing grease& oil from paint without harming the paint.<P>    Next is 3M adhesive remover slightly stronger but still usually doesn't hurt the paint.3M more than likely made the glue so they do a pretty good job getting it off too.<P><BR>   Next is any one of the Citrus based removers found in the sign industry(any good sign shop should have these on hand). Kinda like super goo gone.Be carefull this will eat many paints if left on to long.I've used it on vinyl logos to strip the whole thing without the hassle of scraping off the vinyle then removing the glue just take it all at once..Like I said just be carefull it will eat just about anything if given enough time.(first bottle I orderd was shipped in packing peanuts and leaked...I recieved a wet box of orange scented slime with an empty plastic bottle in it.)<P><BR>                            Greg<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||stsmithgt||10-27-2000||09:48 AM||||Now that it's Friday, I'll have time to search for some of these products.  There ought to be a auto paint supply shop around here somewhere. So many suggestions, so little time.  Time to call in sick and take the day off. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> If its not raining that is.||||reg||1
